{"step_index":1307,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T12:18:31Z","input_tokens":210885,"cache_read_tokens":28279,"output_tokens":355,"content":"That makes the entire security and governance architecture completely coherent:\n\n### The Chatbot Root & Delegation Contract\n\n1. **Root Identity, Zero Direct Execution**:\n   - The **chatbot is the sole root entity** on the system (holding the root session, root WireGuard authority, and control plane access).\n   - However, the chatbot **cannot execute changes directly**. It is strictly a reasoning and delegation controller:\n     - It reads system state, analyzes telemetry, inspects models, and synthesizes solutions.\n     - It has **no ambient execution rights** to directly modify disk, restart services, call outside networks, or change configuration on its own.\n\n2. **Delegation via Bounded Subagents / Workers**:\n   - Any actual execution (e.g. promoting a UI component to the catalog, updating a model route, applying filesystem changes, running commands) **must be delegated** to a specific, sandboxed execution worker or subagent.\n   - The worker runs under an isolated, narrow capability grant specifically bounded to that single task.\n   - Receipts and verification are audited and handed back to the chatbot to review before the action is deemed complete.\n\n3. **User & UI Implications**:\n   - **No Personal Menu**: The chatbot only sees and operates the **Control Plane** (infrastructure, models, tasks, catalog).\n   - **Delegation Trail**: Instead of silent changes, the conversation transcript and audit log reflect explicit delegation steps (propose $\\rightarrow$ delegate $\\rightarrow$ verify $\\rightarrow$ audit receipt).\n\nThis explains why `chat_response` felt completely wrong: it tried to treat the chatbot like a simple text-responder, rather than a **governed root controller that plans and delegates**."}
